---
// /roadmap — what is missing, what comes next, what stays out. Positioning term
// (tests/positioning.spec.ts): "roadmap", in the title, the description and the H1.
// No delivery dates and no promises (the one date is when the roadmap was last read): the order is the maintainer's current intent
// (owner-approved plan, 2026-10-03) and may change.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE } from '../config';

// Edit both by hand, and only after re-reading this page against that release.
const REVIEWED = { version: '0.38', iso: '2026-11-20', label: '20 November 2026' } as const;

const next = [
  { title: 'Moving your domain to Cloudflare, with your assistant', text: 'Many people who start a site already have a domain. Its DNS records, which point it at a website and at mailboxes, sit with another provider. Today a checklist in the toolkit walks you through moving those records to Cloudflare by hand, comparing each one twice. Next, your assistant is to lead that move with you. The plan is written; none of it is built yet.' },
  { title: 'Checks that go deeper and cannot be skipped', text: 'Required checks on the publishing branch, so a failing build cannot go live. Deeper checks on the message of a page. The starter has had a test for leftover template slots and filler text since release 0.30.' },
  { title: 'Listings where people look for plugins', text: "Croftweaver has been a plugin for Claude Code and for Codex since release 0.32, and it is listed on skills.sh. Since release 0.37, what it offers Anthropic's plugin directory is one text-only page that says what Croftweaver is and gives the install commands to type yourself. Next is a listing in OpenAI's own plugin directory." },
  { title: 'Import of an existing site', text: "Bring the content and structure of a site you already have into the Croftweaver starter, under the same tests. Four parts are still to build: taking the content out, including what a count from outside cannot see; bringing it in as pages, with its images; rebuilding the old page layouts and the brand; and settling how the old site's forms and regularly updated records, such as events, work after the move. They will be built during the first real moves, and what a second move repeats goes into Croftweaver." },
];
const later = [
  'A pipeline for regular posts.',
  'Starting from a screenshot or a design file.',
  'A few worked example sites to look at.',
];
const out = [
  { title: 'A visual editor.', text: 'You work with your assistant in plain language.' },
  { title: 'Shops and accounts.', text: 'A shop platform such as Shopify, or a tool that builds and runs web applications, serves you better there.' },
  { title: 'Hosting by us.', text: 'Your site stays on hosting you control.' },
  { title: 'A form that sends through a server.', text: "The visitor's own email program sends the message: it reads like a personal note, they keep a copy, and there is nothing for you to look after." },
];
---
<Base
  title="Roadmap: what is missing, what comes next"
  description="The Croftweaver roadmap: what is missing today, what comes next and in which order, and what stays out on purpose. No delivery dates; none of it is a promise."
  image="/images/og/roadmap.jpg"
>
  <section class="wrap hero">
    <h1>Roadmap: what is missing, what comes next</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      This roadmap lists what Croftweaver does not do yet, the order in which the
      gaps are meant to close, and what will stay out on purpose.
    </p>
    <p class="aside">
      There are no delivery dates here, and none of it is a promise. Croftweaver has one
      maintainer, and the order can change.
    </p>
    <p class="aside" id="reviewed">
      Read through against release {REVIEWED.version} on <time datetime={REVIEWED.iso}>{REVIEWED.label}</time>.
    </p>
    <div class="cta">
      <a class="btn primary" href={SITE.repo}>Get Croftweaver on GitHub</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" id="missing">
    <h2>Missing today</h2>
    <ul class="steps">
      <li><strong>Proof from more than one site.</strong> <a href="/proof">The proof page</a> shows one.</li>
      <li><strong>A lock on publishing.</strong> The tests run by default, but a person can still publish a build that failed. <a href="/checks">The test gate</a> says where.</li>
      <li><strong>Import.</strong> Croftweaver cannot yet bring the content and structure of an existing site into its starter. For WordPress, a helper counts what a site holds before a move, writes the forwarding from your decisions, and after the move checks each old address it found; moving the content itself is still done by hand, with your assistant. For one page at a time, the new-page helper first saves the old page's headings, downloads, forms and embeds, and four weeks after go-live compares the page's clicks before and after. Checking and improving a site where it already lives works today: <a href="/established-sites">Croftweaver for established sites</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Codex and Antigravity on more than one site.</strong> Each has been tested from start to finish on one site; Claude Code is the assistant Croftweaver is developed and used with.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" id="next">
    <h2>Next, in this order</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      {next.map((item) => <li><strong>{item.title}.</strong> {item.text}</li>)}
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" id="done">
    <h2>Done in releases 0.37 and 0.38</h2>
    <ul class="steps">
      <li><strong>New pages that arrive linked and ready for AI answers.</strong> After your assistant builds a new page, it checks that an AI answer can find and quote it, and that at least two pages link to it, one of them inside the text of a page. It also checks that the page links back to its section's main page and is at most three clicks from your home page, and fixes what it finds in the same change. The page brief has a new Links field, so you approve which pages will link to the new page, and the sentence each gets.</li>
      <li><strong>A check of your approved words that reads whole sentences.</strong> A link with a small icon, or a hidden "(opens in a new tab)" note, no longer cuts a sentence into false changes.</li>
      <li><strong>Tests that say what is wrong.</strong> If your site is not built yet, or its address is missing, <code>npm test</code> says so in one line.</li>
      <li><strong>A link check that stays on your side.</strong> It no longer lists your 404 page as a lost page, and it stops with a clear message when the build failed. Pages you leave unlinked on purpose, such as an ad's landing page, go in a plain list, <code>orphan-exempt.txt</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Publishing that reads your settings right.</strong> <code>npm run ship</code> checks the address your site really uses, says which settings file it read, and says what happens if GitHub refuses the push. The build also spots Astro's tab icon after an optimizer has rewritten it.</li>
      <li><strong>A signpost for Anthropic's plugin directory.</strong> What Croftweaver offers the directory is one short page with no code. Your kit, and how you install it, stay as they are.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The rest of what the releases brought is in the release notes for
      <a href={`${SITE.repo}/releases/tag/v0.38`}>0.38</a> and
      <a href={`${SITE.repo}/releases/tag/v0.37`}>0.37</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" id="later">
    <h2>Later, perhaps</h2>
    <ul class="steps">
      {later.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" id="out">
    <h2>Out, on purpose</h2>
    <ul class="steps">
      {out.map((item) => <li><strong>{item.title}</strong> {item.text}</li>)}
    </ul>
    <p>
      If one of these is what you need, <a href="/compare">the alternatives</a> page
      says which kind of tool fits. To argue for a change of order, open an issue
      on <a href={`${SITE.repo}/issues`}>GitHub</a>.
    </p>
    <div class="cta">
      <a class="btn primary" href={SITE.repo}>Get Croftweaver on GitHub</a>
      <a class="btn" href="/start">Quickstart</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  section.wrap { padding: 2.5rem 1.25rem; max-width: var(--maxw); margin: 0 auto; }
  section.hero { padding-top: 3rem; }
  .hero h1 { font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3rem); line-height: 1.1; margin: 0 0 1rem; hyphens: manual; }
  h2 { margin-bottom: 0.8rem; line-height: 1.25; }
  section p, .steps { max-width: 46rem; line-height: 1.7; hyphens: manual; }
  .lead { font-size: 1.15rem; color: var(--muted); }
  .steps { padding-left: 1.3rem; }
  .steps li { margin-bottom: 0.6rem; }
</style>
